Osteoarthritis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Pain worsening with use, Heberden's/Bouchard's nodes, Osteophytes/joint space narrowing

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-031
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, localized mechanical knee pain resulting in sudden functional immobilization, inability to weight-bear safely, and high risk of falls in the home environment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient experiencing acute, severe functional decline due to uncontrolled joint pain, completely unable to ambulate to the bathroom or manage basic needs. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was required to definitively exclude an acute superimposed septic arthritis or crystal arthropathy, and to prescribe robust, immediate immobilizing analgesia, as delaying until standard hours posed a significant risk of an unobserved fall and profound distress.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My right knee has completely given out; the deep grinding ache is so bad I cannot even stand up to get a glass of water."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of a deep, aching joint pain in the right knee that characteristically worsens with use and significantly improves with rest. Over the past 48 hours, following an unusually long walk, this pain acutely flared. The patient describes a sharp, catching sensation superimposed on the chronic ache, severely limiting weight-bearing. Notes minimal morning stiffness today lasting less than 20 minutes before transitioning to mechanical pain on movement. Reports an insidious, asymmetrical involvement of large weight-bearing joints, with the right hip also occasionally aching. Denies fevers, rigors, or recent trauma.
Functional Status: Currently chair-bound; utilizing a broom handle to attempt transfers due to severe mechanical knee pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of falls due to right leg giving way.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Patient is seated, appearing uncomfortable when attempting to shift weight, but not systemically toxic.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal: The right knee exhibits firm, bony enlargement without significant warmth or bright erythema. A small, cool, non-tense effusion is present. There is distinct joint line tenderness, particularly over the medial compartment. Audible and palpable crepitus is pronounced during active and passive range of motion. Passive range of motion is restricted to 100 degrees of flexion due to mechanical block and pain. Hands demonstrate classic Heberden's nodes at the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints and Bouchard's nodes at the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints bilaterally. The diagnostic tetrad is completely aligned: chronic pain worsening with use, presence of Heberden's/Bouchard's nodes, with high clinical suspicion for subchondral sclerosis, osteophytes, and joint space narrowing on plain radiography.
Environmental Observations: Throw rugs present in the living area; advised immediate removal to mitigate trip hazards given current mobility impairment.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking regular paracetamol with inadequate relief; ceased NSAIDs previously due to mild dyspepsia.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Osteoarthritis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute mechanical flare)
Differentials:

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of systemic inflammatory signs (no fever, no rigors), the joint is cool to the touch rather than intensely hot, and the effusion is small and non-tense.

Calcium Pyrophosphate Deposition (Pseudogout) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The gradual mechanical flare rather than a hyper-acute, intensely inflammatory red/hot presentation, though CPPD can coexist with severe OA.

Meniscal Tear - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of widespread polyarticular bony changes (nodes) and chronic crepitus, though an acute degenerative meniscal flap tear may be contributing to the acute mechanical "catching" sensation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Fall risk and immobility)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severely limiting mechanical flare of established osteoarthritis, resulting in sudden immobility and fall risk. Differentiating this from an acute septic or crystalline flare was necessary before initiating therapy. The clinical exam confirmed a cool, mechanically restricted joint with bony hypertrophy, lacking the hallmarks of an acute infection. The patient's inability to safely mobilize for basic physiological needs required an urgent after-hours assessment to provide localized and systemic analgesia and a functional safety plan. Leaving the patient unassessed overnight carried a high probability of a fall with subsequent fracture, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe a short course of a COX-2 selective NSAID (e.g., Celecoxib) combined with a proton pump inhibitor to manage the acute inflammatory component of the mechanical flare while minimizing gastrointestinal risk. Recommend immediate utilization of a robust mobility aid (four-wheel walker) until the acute pain subsides.
Positive Behavioral Change: Weight offloading: Utilize a walking frame for all indoor mobilization over the next 72 hours; observing that bearing weight through the arms immediately prevents the sharp, catching knee pain will powerfully reinforce the necessity of joint protection during a flare. Isometric strengthening: Perform straight-leg raises while seated, holding for 5 seconds, 10 times daily; executing this specific movement strengthens the quadriceps without grinding the joint surfaces, directly proving that muscle activation can occur without exacerbating mechanical joint pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral for graded quadriceps strengthening once the acute flare settles.
DME/Safety (Optional): Four-wheel walker.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to organize updated weight-bearing radiographs.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or regular GP urgently if the knee suddenly becomes bright red, intensely hot to touch, massively swollen, or if you develop a high fever and shaking chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This flare is due to the mechanical wear and tear on your joint cartilage. While the joint is significantly irritated, there are no signs of a dangerous joint infection.
Clinical Course: The acute, sharp pain usually settles over 5 to 7 days with rest, anti-inflammatories, and the use of a walking aid. The chronic ache will persist but should return to your manageable baseline.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk without your frame until the sharp pain subsides. If the joint blows up quickly, turns red and hot, or you start shivering with a fever, you must seek emergency medical help to rule out an infection.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The cartilage cushioning your knee has worn down, causing the bones to rub and the joint lining to become inflamed after your long walk. The prescribed medication specifically targets this inflammation without irritating your stomach as much as standard anti-inflammatories.
Actionable Steps: Use the walking frame at all times. Take the Celecoxib with food. Perform the seated straight-leg raises to keep your thigh muscles from wasting away while you are resting the knee.

